import OddPerfect.EqualExponentSupportSplit

/-!
# Equal exponents: support certificates that split on the special prime (data)

For each prime `l` in 139, 151, 163, 167, 191, 199, 211, 227, 229, 239, 251, 257, 277, 283, 307,
311, 331 a certificate accepted by `EqSupport.splitCheck` with target count `7` (see
`OddPerfect.EqualExponentSupportSplit`): a main case, in which the special prime is none of the
listed excluded primes, and one case for each excluded prime `Q`, in which the special prime is
`Q`. No bound on the special prime is needed. The certificates come from a search outside Lean;
for 227 and 307 the search used factors of the row of `l` from a public factor table, which need
no trust because the checker tests divisibility. Only the kernel check below is trusted. Data
module: it proves nothing about odd perfect numbers alone.

Restricted result; no published source was checked; novelty not verified.
-/

namespace OddPerfect.EqSupport

open OddPerfect.EqCount (big)

